module muster_module {
  version "0.1.0"
  description "Muster — local-first client for coordinating multi-party transactions inside conversations. The coordination surface: propose an intent from an effect, read the exact bytes to sign (the driver-canonicalized materialization), approve it with owner signatures until the driver's threshold is met, and read its lifecycle state. The Nim core behind this contract drives the intent lifecycle engine over a real driver (Safe: EIP-712 safeTxHash + secp256k1 owner recovery). This .lidl stays the single normative surface; the Nim module surface is generated from it by the shared logos_sdk generator (github.com/corpetty/logos-nim-sdk)."

  method health() -> tstr description "Liveness probe. Returns \"ok\" when the module is loaded and dispatching."

  method identity() -> tstr description "Return this module's persistent coordination identity, as JSON: {address, ed25519, x25519, btcPubKey, btcPayers:[{network, chain, address}]}. Two bound keypairs minted on first run and persisted (FS-4) under the host-provided instance path, stable across restarts (F-14, ADR-010 revisit): the secp256k1 authorization key (address; btcPubKey, its compressed public half, which a Bitcoin multisig names you by) and the Ed25519/X25519 encryption identity the room encrypts to. btcPayers is wpkh(btcPubKey) on each Bitcoin network: the address a Bitcoin split's share is paid from and paid at, which your own node must hold coins at (exo-408). A UI shows this as \"who am I in the room\"."

  method describe() -> tstr description "Return the coordination account this module is configured against, as JSON: {chainId, safe, threshold, owners, environment}. Every intent's safeTxHash commits to chainId+safe, so a UI shows the domain it was told rather than one it assumed."

  method settings() -> tstr description "The user-configurable infrastructure this module points at, as JSON {rpc, btcRpc, lez {rpc, chain, multisigProgram}, delivery, environment, identity}: the RPC endpoint the wallet/Safe path reads against, the user's Bitcoin node (credentials redacted, exo-a50.2.6), the delivery createNode config (store/bootstrap nodes) the node boots with at the next start (one node per run), the environment, and the module identity. Untrusted, user-chosen infrastructure (invariant 8) — a settings surface shows and edits these."

  method set_setting(key: tstr, value: tstr) -> tstr description "Set one setting by key: \"rpc\" repoints the wallet/Safe RPC endpoint (the wallet re-inits on next use), \"delivery\" changes the createNode config the NEXT coordinate_join boots with (existing sessions keep their node). \"lez-rpc\" / \"lez-chain\" / \"lez-multisig-program\" point at the user's LEZ sequencer (default https://testnet.lez.logos.co), the zone it serves (CAIP-2, default lez:testnet), and the multisig program (the deployed build's image id, hex; exo-3c9). \"identity-backend\" is \"local\" (the identity in muster's own keyfile) or \"platform:<Ed25519 public key>\" (the person's keystore_module holds it, reached through signer_manager_module; set by identity_platform_status, and only while no room is joined; exo-833). \"support-prompt\" is \"dismissed\" once the person closed the Like Muster? prompt (\"\" shows it again; exo-168). \"btc-rpc\" points at the user's Bitcoin node (http://user:pass@host:port — the credentials travel as Basic auth and are never shown back; exo-a50.2.6). Returns the updated settings, or {error} for an unknown key. In-memory today; a real deployment persists these beside the keystore (and inside basecamp reads them from platform settings)."

  method propose(effect_json: tstr) -> tstr description "Propose an intent from an effect (a JSON object, e.g. {\"to\":\"0x..\",\"value\":1000,\"nonce\":0}). Returns the new intent's id."

  method txhash(intent_id: tstr) -> tstr description "Return the hex materialization the driver canonicalized for this intent — the exact bytes owners sign (for Safe, the EIP-712 safeTxHash)."

  method approve(intent_id: tstr, signature_hex: tstr) -> tstr description "Add an owner signature (65-byte hex) to an intent; the driver verifies it recovers to a configured owner before it counts. Returns the intent's new lifecycle state, or \"rejected\" if the signature does not recover to an owner (or was already counted) — a rejected signature never advances the intent."

  method status(intent_id: tstr) -> tstr description "Return an intent's current lifecycle state (draft, proposed, collecting, executable, ...)."

  method submit(intent_id: tstr) -> tstr description "Submit an executable intent on-chain: assemble the Safe execTransaction from the collected owner signatures, send it through the user's RPC, and watch the receipt. Finality is observed from the chain, never asserted by a service. Returns the intent's new lifecycle state (submitted, then final on a confirmed receipt)."

  method coordinate_join(topic: tstr) -> tstr description "Join (or start) a coordination conversation on a content topic, over encrypted transport. The room's encryption identity is this module's persistent key (see identity()). The room is remembered beside the keystore, so coordinate_start_inbox re-enters it after a relaunch (exo-ecbe). Returns JSON {address, topic}. This is the multi-party counterpart to propose/approve: instead of one client driving the whole lifecycle locally, participants converge on shared intents by folding a signed, encrypted log (state = reduce(log))."

  method coordinate_propose(effect_json: tstr) -> tstr description "Propose an intent into the joined conversation. The intent id is content-addressed from the effect, so every participant derives the same id without coordinating. Publishes a sealed propose event to the room and returns the intent id."
  method coordinate_reannounce() -> tstr description "Re-publish every still-open intent (its policy declaration, propose, and thread card) into the CURRENT membership epoch, so a member admitted AFTER a proposal was made can fold it and see its card — they cannot read anything sealed to an earlier epoch (F-16). Idempotent: intents are content-addressed, so this only re-seals the same events under the new epoch. Call it right after admitting someone. Finished intents (submitted/settled) are skipped. Returns JSON {reannounced: <count>}."

  method coordinate_contribute(intent_id: tstr, signature_hex: tstr, key_ref: tstr) -> tstr description "Contribute an owner signature to a proposed intent in the joined conversation. If signature_hex is EMPTY, sign IN-APP with the keystore: key_ref selects WHICH held key signs (Keystore.signWith, exo-45e K2b/K5) — so one instance holding several owner keys contributes as each, enough of a k-of-n by itself; an empty key_ref uses the primary key (backward-compatible), an unknown ref returns \"unknown-key\". A pasted signature_hex is used as-is. The signature is verified to recover to a configured owner/roster member before it is published (keyed by the recovered contributor, so a non-owner is \"rejected\" and a duplicate folds once). The secret never leaves the keystore. Returns the intent's new lifecycle state as folded from the shared log."

  method coordinate_intents() -> tstr description "List the intents in the joined conversation as folded from the shared log: a JSON array of {id, state, ...render fields, n, profile}, where profile is the governing driver's family profile for its instance (docs/design/multisig-landscape.md: family id, locus, scheme, binding, ordering, expiry, what the chain learns, CAIP-2 chain, CAIP-10 account, k, n, whether the ways around the threshold are known). Drives inbound delivery first, so it reflects contributions that have arrived from other participants."

  method coordinate_readiness(intent_id: tstr) -> tstr description "The proposal card's five questions for one room intent, plus THIS instance's readiness to take part (docs/design/action-manifest.md, exo-002.2). Returns JSON {intentId, policy, declared, ready, unknown, items:[{kind, name, party, needs, status, detail, remedy, and on a module item moduleState, install}], manifest:{agreement:{rounds, threshold, finality, domain}, requirements, discloses:[{field, to}] (the full disclosure — baseline rows to the room and the store node included, FS-9), touches:[{target, mode}]}, profile:{family, locus, scheme, ..., chain, account, k, n, bypassesKnown}}. status is met | missing | unknown per requirement, graded against this instance only: a module requirement asks the host's module registry first (modules_state.module_record, bounded and cached) whether it is installed, starting, ready or failed, and calls into the module itself only once the host reports it ready, or when there is no registry to ask — its item also carries moduleState (ready | starting | installed | not-installed | error | unknown) and install, the catalogue package to install when installing would help (not met, and not-installed or unknown; the driver may name a package other than the module, else the module's own name; empty otherwise), and each module requirement in manifest.requirements carries its install package; an environment requirement probes the configured RPC for the expected chain, an authority requirement checks whether YOUR key is a recognized signer (never who else is, invariant 9), an infra requirement checks configuration, a capability requirement is unknown until the host broker exists. unknown is a first-class answer, never a silent met; an undeclared manifest yields declared:false and ready:false. The client renders install / authorize from the remedies — an install through Basecamp's packages.install intent, on the person's confirmation; the module itself installs nothing (invariant 3)."

  method coordinate_decline(intent_id: tstr) -> tstr description "Decline to take part in a proposed room intent (the card's Deny, exo-002.3). Publishes a decline event keyed by this member so the room sees who is out. A decline is final (exo-720): this member's approval counts toward nothing, whichever arrived first, and once too many of those the driver names as signers have declined for the threshold to be reached the intent is declined. Once the room has submitted the intent, a decline changes nothing. Folds once per member. Returns JSON {intentId, state, declines} or {error}."

  method coordinate_offers(intent_id: tstr) -> tstr description "The card's 'From you' section for one room intent (exo-45e K4/K6, docs/design/material-and-disclosure.md §3.3): which of MY OWN holdings can fill the slots this proposal asks of me — the contributor keys and any counterparty address/asset the effect still needs. Returns JSON {intentId, ready, offers:[{requirement:{kind,name,party,field}, status, candidates:[{public, class, form, grade, discloses:[{field,to}]}]}]}. Each candidate is the PUBLIC face of a holding (never a handle, s1) with its F-10 grade and the disclosure rows choosing it would ADD (s6) — never a score or rank (s7). Graded about ME only: it reads only my own catalogue and never names, or is changed by, another member's holdings (invariant 9, s3). status is satisfiable | unsatisfiable | unknown (a host capability is unknown until the broker); ready is true only when every slot has one of my holdings."

  method compose_offers(effect_json: tstr) -> tstr description "The composer's account step (F-18 step three, exo-45e K6): for a DRAFT effect under the room's current policy, which of my holdings can fill the PROPOSER slots — the source account, the asset+amount, a destination I supply directly. Same payload shape as coordinate_offers {ready, offers:[...]}, computed about me only. The client greys an action whose proposer slots I cannot fill (naming the missing slot) rather than hiding it, so I see what one more holding would let me do."

  method coordinate_share_material(intent_id: tstr, requirement: tstr, public_face: tstr) -> tstr description "Share one of MY holdings into a room intent to fill a slot it asks of me (exo-45e K5/K6): the counterparty address a payment needs, say. `public` is the PUBLIC face of a candidate from coordinate_offers (the handle never left the instance, s1); only that public face, class and form are published (never the handle, s1), bound to the intent and the effect field the requirement lands in — the request-first path by which a complete effect gets its counterparty material. Publishes a material-share event keyed by this member so the room sees who shared; it folds once per (requirement, sharer) and the flow view then shows the shared value disclosed to the room, the outside rows waiting for submit (s6). Returns JSON {intentId, requirement, field, public} or {error}."

  method coordinate_provenance() -> tstr description "Provenance for EVERY action in the joined room (docs/design/action-manifest.md M4, exo-002.4): the log's lineage folded in canonical order as a JSON array of {seq, class, kind, intentId, account, alias, accountable, what, detail, guarantee, epoch}. class is the F-20 vocabulary (peer-message / driver-contribution / external-read / plugin-block); kind is message / propose / policy / sig / decline / submit / final / admit; guarantee says what the code actually enforces for that entry (a message's author signed it, bound to the room, and the room's views drop one whose signature fails (exo-f76); an approval's signer is what its driver verified; submit/final are external reads). epoch is the membership epoch the entry belongs to. Drives inbound delivery first."

  method coordinate_proof() -> tstr description "An exportable, self-verifying proof of the joined room's log (M4): JSON {format: muster.log-proof.v1, events, ids, digest, epochFrom, epochTo, proofDigest} — the event set in canonical order with each event's content id, the state digest reduce(events) reaches, and the membership epochs it spans. Anyone holding those epoch keys can check it with coordinate_verify_proof; nobody else can read it, let alone forge it (invariant 7). Proves the log, not the world: chain results stay external reads. Sharing a proof OUTSIDE the room is selective disclosure of the log — the member's own choice, never the client's."

  method coordinate_verify_proof(proof_json: tstr) -> tstr description "Verify a log proof (from coordinate_proof, this room or another): recompute every event's content id, require the slice to be parent-closed (no event dropped from the middle of a chain), in canonical order, reaching its claimed state digest. Returns JSON {ok, reason, proofDigest, events} — refuse-on-mismatch, the first discrepancy named. Pure: it reads nothing but the proof."
  method coordinate_audit(intent_id: tstr) -> tstr description "The signature-audit file for one room intent (exo-403, derived-exo-403): one canonical-CBOR record of everything its approvals cover — the effect and its re-derived materialization, the signing context, the provenance record and the parent-closed lineage it names, every approval with its grade (committed: a verified muster attestation; unattested: signed outside muster and pasted; rejected: an attestation that does not verify) and its signature + attestation events, the settlement entries graded as external reads with the tx hash, and the claims the log alone cannot prove (threshold, stage, disclosure, first epoch) signed by THIS member. Returns JSON {ok, reason, file (hex), report (Markdown rendered from the file, carrying its digest), digest}. Refused — never truncated — when the intent's history reaches an event this member cannot read (an earlier epoch). Exporting writes nothing; sharing the file outside the room is the member's own choice (it discloses what they already hold)."

  method coordinate_verify_audit(file_hex: tstr) -> tstr description "Verify a signature-audit file (from coordinate_audit, any room): re-derives the materialization, the context, the inputs and the signing payload from the file alone; recomputes every content id, the canonical order and the parent closure; checks each approval is signed by the contributor it names and recomputes its grade; checks the exporter's signature over the non-derivable claims. Returns JSON {ok, reason, intentId, stage, issuer, digest, firstEpoch, approvals:[{who, round, grade}], settlement:[{kind, chainRef, grade: external-read, part?, author?}]} — kind is submit / final, or for a split part-settled / part-confirmed, each by the member its driver names for that part (the payer's report that it paid, the creditor's own read that it arrived) — refuse-on-mismatch, the first discrepancy named. Pure: it reads nothing but the file; settlement is never reported as verified against the chain."


  method coordinate_flow() -> tstr description "The information-flow view of the joined room (docs/design/action-manifest.md M5, exo-002.5): who could see what, for every action, folded from the log × each action's manifest disclosure × the membership at that point. Returns JSON {rows:[{seq, kind, intentId, field, to, members, epoch, declared}], matrix:{<observer>: [fields]}}. Inside-the-boundary rows list the members who held the epoch key at that position (founders + joiners admitted by then); the store node's metadata rows appear on EVERY entry (FS-9); a driver's declared outside rows (chain observer, RPC provider, target module) appear at submit/final — when the information actually leaves. An undeclared driver yields an 'undeclared' row at the boundary, never silence. The room's shared truth, never a per-viewer claim. Drives inbound delivery first."

  method coordinate_graph(room_topic: tstr) -> tstr description "The room map of a joined room (docs/design/room-map.md, epic exo-dde): room_topic names the room (any spelling coordinate_join accepts); empty means the current room, and a room this instance has not joined returns {error: not-joined}. the room as nodes and edges, folded from the log, adding no truth of its own. Returns JSON {viewer, readFrom, seqMax, epochs:[{epoch, fromSeq, admitted}], nodes:[{id, kind, seq, epoch, attrs}], edges:[{src, dst, kind, seq, attrs}]}. Node kinds: entry (every log entry this instance holds, keyed e:<content id>, with its provenance kind, class, guarantee and attribution basis), member, signer (an approval key no verified binding ties to a member), intent, account, ceremony, observer, outside. Edge kinds: parent (the hash link), authored, bound, proposed, approved, declined, part, input, disclosed, joined, leaves (what left to an outside observer, with its fields, at the entry where it left), enters (a read the room took in). Every node and edge carries its position in causal order (the order members published), so a view replays the room by filtering on seq. Every entry carries facets and every edge a credibility (imperative / motivational / exposed / not-applicable, the party, the mechanism: what the code enforces, never a score); crossings lists what left the room, what came in and who joined, in order; fingerprints gives, per epoch, a digest of the entries held from then on that two members can compare; dropped counts entries the room refused because their author signature failed. The facets and guarantee every entry of a kind shares are sent once: facetSets and texts list them, and an attr's facets or guarantee holding a number is an index into them (exo-dde.19). Content included: the map is local, so each entry carries its value parsed (message text, the effect, context, read values, signatures, chain references, payment reports, material, account details), each proposal its effect, summary and materialization hash, and each crossing the values that left and the chain reference that sent them — never anything this instance's session did not decrypt. An actor is named only where the log proves it. Two members' maps of one log differ only in viewer and readFrom, and a later joiner's map holds nothing from before its admission. Drives inbound delivery first."

  method coordinate_authorization(intent_id: tstr) -> tstr description "The muster-issued authorization for one room intent (docs/design/host-effect-policy.md, exo-002.7): the grant a host hook checks before it lets a gated module call through. Issued ONLY for an intent the room has folded to executable — {error: not-executable} otherwise, because nothing is authorized before agreement. Returns JSON {format: muster.authorization.v1, intentId, capability, materializationRoot, environment, account, slot (= intentId), expiry, issuer, signature, digest}: the capability names the action (invoke → module.method; safe → safe.execute; the room-native drivers → room.attest), the root is the exact materialization the room agreed on, the context is replay-bound (invariant 2), and the signature is this instance's secp256k1 authorization identity over the domain-separated digest. The plugin dispatches nothing (invariant 3); the host does, after coordinate_check_authorization (or its own equivalent) passes."

  method coordinate_check_authorization(auth_json: tstr) -> tstr description "Pure, refuse-on-mismatch check of a muster authorization (what the host hook runs, or any member can run): recover the issuer from the signature over the domain-separated digest, require slot == intentId and now <= expiry. Returns JSON {ok, reason, issuer, capability, materializationRoot, expiry}. The host additionally supplies the root of the call it is about to dispatch and the issuers its policy trusts; this method checks the grant's own integrity."

  method coordinate_activity() -> tstr description "The room's coordination history as reduce(log): every state transition that moved a proposal along — proposed, each approval (with the running count, and who), threshold reached, submitted on-chain, settled — in canonical (causal) order. The education seam: a member sees how the room reached its state and watches it change as it happens, from the SAME sealed log the cards are drawn from, inventing nothing. Returns a JSON array of {seq, kind, intentId, account, title, detail}. Drives inbound delivery first, so it reflects events from other participants."

  method connectivity() -> tstr description "Liveness of the infrastructure the room relies on, and only that (invariant 8: store nodes and RPC are untrusted, user-chosen infra, so their reachability must be visible, never assumed; exo-428: infrastructure is dictated by drivers). The room's baseline is its delivery node (getNodeInfo). Every other row is introduced by a proposal whose driver manifest declares an instance-party infra/environment requirement, folded from the log: a Safe proposal introduces the RPC (eth_chainId, checked against the chain it needs); a room with no such proposal never probes an RPC; an undeclared driver shows as unknown. Returns JSON {rows:[{key,name,level,detail,source,endpoint?,remedy?,introducedBy:[{intentId,policy}]}]} where level is ok, warn (reachable but not what the proposal needs), down, or unknown, and source is room or proposal. Never a false green."
  method rln_status() -> tstr description "This node's RLN membership (exo-eb6.3), as the connectivity panel's row: {key:\"rln\", name, level, detail, remedy?, payer?, balance?}. Off logos.test, the one preset that runs RLN, the row says it is not needed. On logos.test a delivery v0.3 node sends only with an active membership; the RLN modules provision the node's own wallet on the registry's zone and register by themselves once its payer is funded, so the row names the payer and what to send while it waits. Reads the node's RLN state from delivery and the RLN modules' own replies; never a chain write, and nothing unread reads as ok."
  method keystore_status() -> tstr description "The official EVM keystore (keystore_module, Basecamp 0.3.1's catalog; exo-149.1 K1) as a status row: {key:\"keystore\", name, level, detail, remedy?, identity?:{kind, identity}, approvers:[...], accounts:[{address, label, wallet}]}. keystore_module holds the EVM keys; muster may read its accounts and request signatures a human approves, never sign itself. ok only when keystore_module attributes muster's calls to muster_module (caller_identity kind module, identity muster_module) and names an approver; any other caller kind, or another module's name, is down because every request would be refused; no approver or no account is warn with the remedy; not loaded, or a reply unread or failed, is down. Every read is ungated and local, made async, at most every 5 s; never a chain read, never a signature, and nothing unread reads as ok."
  method keystore_requests() -> tstr description "The in-room approvals this member has asked keystore_module for (exo-149.2 K2), open or just finished: {backend: off|interim, requests:[{handle, intentId, account, state: waiting|shown|approved|rejected|expired|cancelled|failed, reason, legs, published?}]}. A coordinate_contribute whose key ref names a keystore_module account (setting keystore-backend = interim) returns awaiting-approval and appears here; a human approves it in an approver (evm_signer_ui, raised with evm.signing.approve {handle}; or evm_signer_cli headless); the intents tick then fetches the signatures, checks each recovers to the account over muster's own hash, and publishes through the same gates as an in-app approval (published = the intent's state then, or a refusal). Never a receipt."
  method keystore_select(address: tstr) -> tstr description "Approve this member's Safe intents with a keystore_module account (exo-149.5 K5); \"\" stops. Needs setting keystore-backend = interim and an account keystore_module lists. Selecting asks keystore_module, once, for the account's F-14 binding (a human approves it in an approver; it appears in keystore_requests as kind binding), which is then published beside each approval so every member's view counts it as this member's; it is valid 30 days. From then, coordinate_contribute on a Safe intent with no key ref is approved through this account. Returns {ok, account, handle?, state?} or {ok:false, error}. keystore_status reports the selection and the binding's state (none | valid | expiring | expired | invalid)."
  method identity_platform_request() -> tstr description "Ask the person's wallet for a room identity of muster's own (exo-833): signer_manager_module request_identity {purpose room-identity, context default, unlock {apps [muster_module], confirm false}}, which the person approves in the keys app; the key is an Ed25519 key in keystore_module, from the recovery phrase it keeps, derived for muster_module/default so no other module can obtain it, and unlocked for muster so room messages need no prompt each. Returns {ok, handle, kind: identity, state} (the handle is what the keys app shows: raise keystore.accounts.open {handle}), {ok:false, error, handle} while another ask waits, or {ok:false, error, install?} when signer_manager_module is not loaded. The receipt stays in this process; identity_platform_status collects the result."
  method identity_platform_unlock() -> tstr description "Ask the person to open muster's platform identity for muster again once its unlock ran out (exo-833): signer_manager_module request_unlock {account, apps [muster_module], confirm false} for the account identity_platform_request was given, decided in the keys app like the request itself. Returns {ok, handle, kind: unlock, state}, or {ok:false, error} when the identity is muster's own or no account is known for it. identity_platform_status says when it is done."
  method identity_platform_status() -> tstr description "The platform identity and the ask in flight (exo-833). Always: backend, locked (the last identity request was refused unsettled because muster's unlock is gone; ask identity_platform_unlock), changeable (no room joined yet, so the backend may change), and with a platform backend its publicKey and account. Then state: none with nothing asked; waiting {kind, handle, step} until the person decides; ended {kind, reason} once when it settled with nothing to collect (rejected, cancelled, expired_no_ack); ready for an unlock {unlocked}; ready for an identity {publicKey, account, context, unlocked, applied, detail}: whether setting identity-backend = platform:<publicKey> took effect. It takes effect only while this instance has joined no room (each room knows a member by its identity; detail says so otherwise). From then the room identity is that key: authorship, join requests and after-claims are signed through the manager as records it shows, sealed grants and the founding key open through it, and a signature over raw bytes is refused."

  method coordinate_account() -> tstr description "The room's sending context for the composer — WHAT an intent proposed here would move and WHO you act as (ask-then-disclose: surfaced at propose time, not assumed on entry). For a Safe room returns JSON {policy, account (the Safe), actingAs (your owner address), isOwner (whether your identity recovers to a configured Safe owner — so you learn before proposing whether your approval will count on-chain), assets:[{symbol, decimals, display, raw} or {..., error}]} where assets is the Safe's live balance — what is available to send. A non-Safe policy settles nothing on-chain, so it returns just {policy}. Never a false balance: an unreachable RPC surfaces an error, not a zero."

  method coordinate_submit(intent_id: tstr) -> tstr description "Settle a room intent on-chain FROM the room. For a Safe-policy intent that has reached executable (its threshold of owner signatures folded from the shared log), re-derive the safeTxHash, assemble the Safe execTransaction from those exact signatures, submit it through the user's RPC, and observe finality from the receipt (R-8, never asserted). Publishes a submit event so every member's fold converges on submitted. Returns JSON {state, onchain, txHash} where onchain is final/pending/failed — or {error} for a non-executable intent, a non-Safe policy (a threshold endorsement settles nothing on-chain), or an unreachable RPC. This is the room-side counterpart to submit(); the coordinated signatures come from the log, not local state."

  method coordinate_execute(intent_id: tstr) -> tstr description "Execute a generic module-action intent FROM the room (the invoke-policy counterpart to coordinate_submit). For an invoke-policy intent that reached executable (the room endorsed it k-of-n, folded from the shared log), re-derive the materialization (invariant 1), read the module/method/args the effect names, and — gated by the ALLOWLIST (only configured module.method pairs are executed) AND the target's own CAPABILITY policy (enforced by the lp_* layer; a denied call is a refusal, never a false success) — invoke module.method(args). Publishes submit + (on immediate completion) final events so every member's fold converges. Returns JSON {state, executed, detail} — or {error} for a non-executable intent or a non-invoke policy. The driver never invokes: the core does (invariant 3), exactly as coordinate_submit does for Safe."
  method coordinate_available_actions() -> tstr description "Discover the coordinatable module actions available to the room (P-D3): the things your loaded modules let you DO together. For each candidate module (the invoke allowlist's modules plus any named in MUSTER_INVOKE_MODULES — muster does not blind-scan every loaded module), query its lp_get_methods descriptors, prune the obvious reads (get*/list*/version/… unless curated in), and return a JSON array [{module, method, signature, params, allowed}]. `allowed` marks the actions on the invoke allowlist (executable now, gated further by the target's capability policy) versus candidates an operator could opt in. The client renders this as the compose menu; picking one composes an invoke intent the room coordinates and coordinate_execute runs."
  method coordinate_export_outside(intent_id: tstr) -> tstr description "A room intent in its driver's OUTSIDE-SIGNER format (seam S8, exo-a50.2.6), for a signer outside muster — a hardware signer, Sparrow, Bitcoin Core. For a Bitcoin multisig intent: the proposal's spend as a base64 PSBT (every input's prevout, its witnessScript or tapleaf + control block). Nothing is signed or published. Returns JSON {intentId, format, encoded} or {error: unknown-intent | unsupported-driver | no-outside-format} — a driver with no such format says so, never a guess."
  method coordinate_import_outside(intent_id: tstr, encoded: tstr) -> tstr description "What an outside signer returned for a room intent (exo-a50.2.6): the intent's DRIVER reads every signature in it (for Bitcoin, a PSBT: each account key that signed every input) and verifies each like a native one. Each is published as a PASTED approval — it counts toward the threshold and every member sees it graded unattested, signed outside muster, never committed. A response to a different spend is refused whole and nothing is published. Returns JSON {intentId, imported:[signer], already:[signer], state} or {error: not-this-spend | not-readable | no-signatures | expired | rejected, detail}."
  method coordinate_propose_btc_spend(pay_to: tstr, amount_sat: tstr, fee_rate: tstr) -> tstr description "Propose a Bitcoin payment from the room's chosen Bitcoin multisig account (the compose policy, btc-p2wsh@ / btc-tapscript@ / btc-frost@<CAIP-10>, exo-a50.2.6; a FROST account spends by key path, Phase D). The account's coins are read from the user's Bitcoin node (Settings → btc-rpc; scantxoutset — an external read, recorded in the room's log so every signer can account for it, invariant 10); the spend pays amount_sat to pay_to, largest coins first, change back to the account, at fee_rate sat/vB over an upper-bound size. Returns the intent id, or JSON {error: not-joined | not-a-bitcoin-policy | no-bitcoin-node | node-unreachable | cannot-build, detail}."
  method frost_ceremony_open(ceremony_id: tstr, network: tstr, t: tstr, n: tstr) -> tstr description "Open a FROST key ceremony (ChillDKG, Phase D) in the joined room, and join it as a participant. The room is the ceremony's coordinator: every member computes its steps from the log. The first n members to join are the participants. This instance's part advances on the coordinate_intents tick (step 1, step 2, finalize). When it completes, the t-of-n taproot account (btc.frost-bip445) is disclosed into the room with its public recovery data. Returns JSON {ceremony, network, t, n, host} or {error}."
  method frost_ceremony_join(ceremony_id: tstr) -> tstr description "Join an open FROST key ceremony in the joined room with this member's host key for it. The key is derived in the keystore and never exported. The ceremony advances on the coordinate_intents tick. Returns JSON {ceremony, host} or {error: not-joined | not-open}."
  method frost_ceremonies() -> tstr description "The FROST key ceremonies in the joined room, folded from its log: JSON [{ceremony, network, t, n, joined, step1, step2, participant (is this member one), address (once disclosed), last (this member's last step outcome)}]."
  method xmr_msig_setup_open(ceremony_id: tstr, chain: tstr, k: tstr, members_json: tstr) -> tstr description "Open a Monero multisig setup in the joined room (ADR-020, exo-dcc.34.2) and join it. Each member's OWN monero-wallet-rpc (Settings → xmr-msig-rpc) runs the key exchange; the room only carries its messages, in order; Muster holds no Monero key. chain is CAIP-2 (\"\" = stagenet; stagenet only: Monero multisig is experimental and pre-FCMP++); k is M; members_json is a JSON array of room identities (\"\" = the room's current members), the opener among them. No wallet is asked anything until every member has joined; then each member's part advances on the coordinate_intents tick. When every wallet is ready, each member discloses the address its own wallet reports, and the room acts from the account (xmr-msig@<CAIP-10>) only once every member disclosed the same one. Returns JSON {ceremony, chain, k, n} or {error: not-joined | bad-threshold | bad-members | not-a-member | refused, detail}."
  method xmr_msig_setup_join(ceremony_id: tstr) -> tstr description "Consent to take part in an open Monero multisig setup in the joined room (ADR-020): this member's wallet (Settings → xmr-msig-rpc) then runs its rounds of the exchange on the coordinate_intents tick. A wallet already multisig, or on another network, is refused and never run. Returns JSON {ceremony} or {error: not-joined | refused, detail}."
  method xmr_msig_setups() -> tstr description "The Monero multisig setups in the joined room, folded from its log (ADR-020): JSON [{ceremony, chain, k, n, members, joined, rounds (member → rounds its wallet published), mine, joinedByMe, address (once disclosed), disclosedBy, last (this member's last step outcome), wallet (whether xmr-msig-rpc is set)}]."
  method coordinate_propose_xmr_msig(account: tstr, recipients_json: tstr, fee_cap: tstr, memo: tstr) -> tstr description "Propose a spend from a Monero multisig the room set up (ADR-020, exo-dcc.34.3): account is its CAIP-10 id (\"\" = the compose policy's, xmr-msig@…), recipients_json a JSON array of {address, amount} in atomic units (1 XMR = 10^12), fee_cap the most the network fee may be (atomic units). The members agree with their room keys (M of them; the first M are the signers). Then, on the coordinate_intents tick and each from their own wallet (Settings → xmr-msig-rpc): every signer syncs, the first builds the agreed spend, each next signer signs only after its wallet's describe_transfer matches the agreed recipients, change and fee cap, the last submits, and any member's wallet marks it final at 10 confirmations. One spend per wallet is in flight at a time. Stagenet only; Monero transactions carry no expiry. Returns the intent id, or JSON {error: not-joined | no-account | account-not-ready | bad-recipients | refused, detail}."
  method xmr_msig_spends() -> tstr description "The Monero multisig spends in the joined room and where each stands (ADR-020): JSON [{intentId, state, signers (the signer set, once round 1 closed), synced, signed (members whose wallet published its sync data / its transaction set), txids (once submitted), last (this member's last step outcome), wallet (whether xmr-msig-rpc is set)}]."
  method coordinate_vote(intent_id: tstr) -> tstr description "Approve a VOTE-locus intent (a LEZ multisig proposal, exo-3c9) with THIS member's own on-chain vote. It re-reads the proposal from the chain and refuses on any difference from what the room reviewed (S5). It then signs the member's Approve with the member's LEZ key (held in the keystore, never exported) and sends it to the user's sequencer (Settings → lez-rpc). It does not wait on a block: it returns \"pending: …\" at once. A later coordinate_intents tick confirms the vote on chain, and only then publishes the receipt the fold counts, with an attestation by the member's room key; the intent carries chainPending \"vote\" meanwhile. Returns \"pending: …\" or a refusal (not-joined | not-a-vote-locus | refused: …). coordinate_contribute with no signature routes here for a vote-locus intent."
  method coordinate_propose_split(chain: tstr, total: tstr, shares_json: tstr, memo: tstr) -> tstr description "Propose splitting a bill YOU fronted (exo-a90, docs/design/split-the-bill.md): you are the creditor, paid at your own address on chain (CAIP-2, e.g. eip155:31337); total is canonical decimal in the smallest unit (wei). shares_json is a JSON array of room identities (even shares among them and you, rounded down, you absorb the remainder), or {parties: [...], creditorShares: false} (chip in: you take no share), or {shares: [{who, amount}]} (explicit). An object may add fiat: {currency, amount, rate, source} for a bill in another currency (exo-3a4): the rate is this member's quote of the asset per ONE unit of the currency, recorded with the proposal as an external read (invariant 10), and the total is its exact conversion — the total argument is then ignored (error bad-quote when it cannot be converted exactly). An object may add creditor: <room identity> to propose on that member's behalf (exo-770): they are the creditor, paid at the Ethereum address they last shared into the room (error no-shared-address if none; a private split is proposed only by its creditor). The policy is evm-split@<chain> (lez-split, btc-split or monero-split on a lez:, bip122: or monero: chain). Each person named must agree before anyone pays, the creditor too: proposing your own split is your agreement, and a creditor's client refuses to agree to a payTo it does not hold (payto-not-mine). On a monero: chain (exo-dcc.5, ADR-018; \"\" with the compose policy monero-split = stagenet) it is an XMR payment request: total and shares in atomic units (1 XMR = 10^12), even shares made distinct (one subaddress takes them all), no fiat and no token; payTo is a fresh subaddress YOUR open Monero wallet mints for it (create_subaddress, labelled muster:req-…), or on a member's behalf the Monero address they last shared for that chain; errors add no-wallet | wallet-other-network | wallet-watch-only | wallet-unread | wallet-busy, each with detail, request (the intent that opens a wallet, monero.wallet.unlock) and install (monero_wallet_ui). Returns the intent id, or {error}."
  method coordinate_settle_part(intent_id: tstr) -> tstr description "Pay YOUR share of an agreed split from your own wallet (exo-a90.4): the transfer (your share, to the split's payTo, its asset, its chain) is derived from the split everyone agreed to — nothing is typed, nothing else can be sent. Returns {pending: tx} — your settled report follows on the intents tick once the payment lands — or {error: not-agreed | not-a-party | already-settled | expired | …}. A Monero request sends nothing (muster never spends XMR): it returns {payFrom: \"your-wallet\", uri (the monero: link of your agreed share), amount (atomic units), to, chain, detail} to pay from any wallet of yours, then coordinate_report_paid."
  method coordinate_report_paid(intent_id: tstr, tx: tstr) -> tstr description "Say you paid YOUR share of a Monero request (exo-dcc.5) from your own wallet: your author-signed settled report, carrying the transaction id if you give one (64 hex; empty = none). It is your claim, shown as such: it never confirms the part — only the creditor's own wallet does. Returns {state} or {error: not-joined | unknown-intent | not-a-monero-request | not-a-party | not-agreed | already-settled | bad-txid}."
  method coordinate_confirm_part(intent_id: tstr, part: tstr, tx: tstr) -> tstr description "As the creditor, confirm one share of a split (part = the debtor's ed:<hex> name). With tx, only once YOUR OWN RPC shows that transaction paying you exactly the share, and never a transaction already confirmed for another share; with tx empty, the share was received outside muster (your word, shown as such). Your client also confirms reported payments on its own, on the intents tick. A Monero request (exo-dcc.5) is confirmed only from YOUR open wallet's history — an incoming transfer to payTo's subaddress of exactly the share, 10 confirmations deep, not used for another part — on the tick whether or not the debtor reported it; here tx non-empty reads your wallet now, tx empty is Mark received (your word). Returns {state} or {error} (unconfirmed: pending: … while it is not there or your wallet did not answer; unconfirmed: wallet-other-network / no-wallet … with the remedy)."
  method coordinate_propose_settle_up(chain: tstr, asset: tstr, memo: tstr) -> tstr description "Settle up (exo-3c6, docs/design/split-the-bill.md §4.11): net the room's agreed, unpaid split shares on chain (CAIP-2; \"\" = the compose policy's, else the chain your RPC serves) in asset (\"\" = ETH, or BTC on a Bitcoin chain; 0x<token> for a token) into fewer payments — each member's balance conserved, each recipient paid only at an address a split owing them agreed. Every party the covered shares name must agree (proposing is your agreement if you are one); once agreed the covered shares are paid only through it, and once its payments are confirmed each creditor's client marks the shares it covered received. The private split and a Monero split are never netted (not-netted). Returns the intent id, or {error} (nothing-to-net when fewer than two open shares)."
  method coordinate_open_assets() -> tstr description "What a settle-up across assets could cover (exo-a90.17, docs/design/split-the-bill.md §4.13): the room's agreed, unpaid split shares on every public rail (Ethereum and Bitcoin — never the private split), grouped by chain and asset. Returns JSON {assets: [{chain, asset, parts, symbol, decimals}]} — decimals -1 when a token's decimals could not be read through your RPC."
  method coordinate_propose_settle_up_across(chain: tstr, asset: tstr, rates: tstr, memo: tstr) -> tstr description "Settle up across assets and chains (exo-a90.17, docs/design/split-the-bill.md §4.13): paid on chain (CAIP-2; \"\" = the compose policy's, else the chain your RPC serves) in asset (\"\" = ETH, or BTC on a Bitcoin chain; 0x<token> for a token), covering the open shares in that asset and in every other one `rates` prices — a JSON array [{chain, asset, rate, source}], rate the payment asset per ONE unit of that asset as a decimal (\"21.4\"), source where it came from. Each share converts at its rate, rounded down; the rates are YOUR recorded quote (invariant 10), signed, and shown on the card before anyone agrees. A recipient owed by no covered split on the payment chain is paid at the address they last shared for it, and their own client agrees only to an address it holds. Returns the intent id, or {error: nothing-to-net | no-address (who, name: they must share an address on that chain) | no open share in … | unknown-decimals | …}."
  method coordinate_share_address(chain: tstr) -> tstr description "Share YOUR address for a chain into the room (exo-a90.17): your Ethereum address for an eip155 chain, your own Bitcoin address (wpkh of your muster key) on that network for a bip122 one, your shielded key node on that zone for a lez: one (what a private payment sent to you is paid at, exo-dcc.33), a FRESH subaddress your open Monero wallet mints for a monero: one (exo-dcc.5) — an author-signed address-share card, what a split or a settle-up across chains pays you at (a Monero request proposed on your behalf, which your client agrees to only while your wallet lists it). Returns {address} or {error} (a Monero error adds detail and request: monero.wallet.unlock)."
  method coordinate_renew_split(intent_id: tstr) -> tstr description "Renew a split past its expiry (exo-a90.15, docs/design/split-the-bill.md §4.12): past a split's expiry no share of it is paid, so its unpaid shares are renewed as a settle-up of that one split — each unpaid share, exactly as the split says it, paid by its own debtor to the split's payTo, under the renewal's own fresh expiry. Everyone those shares name agrees again (proposing is your agreement if you are one); once it is paid and final, each creditor marks the shares it covered received and the split goes final. Its memo names when it was made, so renewing again after a renewal lapsed is a new intent. Returns the renewal's intent id, or {error: not-joined | unknown-intent | nothing to renew… | not a split | the private split is never netted…}."
  method coordinate_token_info(chain: tstr, token: tstr) -> tstr description "What an ERC-20 token says about itself, for a split paid in it (exo-5ab): read through YOUR RPC when it serves `chain` (a CAIP-2 eip155 id), `token` a 0x address or erc20:0x…. Returns JSON {asset, token, symbol, decimals}, or {error} when your RPC does not serve that chain or the token does not answer symbol()/decimals(). Display only — a split's amounts are base units and nothing here is signed; a token names itself, so show its address beside its symbol."
  method coordinate_propose_lez(action_json: tstr) -> tstr description "Propose an action from the room's chosen LEZ multisig account (compose policy lez-multisig@<CAIP-10>, exo-3c9). action_json is {target (the 32-byte program id, hex), instruction (u32 words), accounts (32-byte account ids, hex), pdaSeeds (hex), authorized (indices into accounts)}. The proposer's own Propose transaction puts it ON CHAIN as the next proposal (signed by their LEZ member key, which is their first vote). It does not wait on a block: it returns JSON {pending: \"propose\", index, tx, detail} at once. A later coordinate_intents tick publishes the room intent pointing at the proposal (with the content the room reviews) once the chain holds it. Otherwise it returns JSON {error: not-joined | not-a-lez-policy | not-a-member | sequencer-unreachable | refused, detail}."
  method coordinate_propose_lez_transfer(recipient: tstr, amount: tstr) -> tstr description "Propose a token transfer from the LEZ multisig's vault (its PDA holding) to recipient (a token holding account, hex or base58): a Transfer to the chain's token program, with the vault as the authorized PDA. Like coordinate_propose_lez otherwise. The vault must already hold the token (see coordinate_propose_lez_vault_init). Under a lez-frost@ policy (a FROST group's own LEZ account, exo-55e) the group sends from its account: a lez-call at the account's nonce read from the user's sequencer (a recorded read), approved in two rounds and settled with one aggregate signature."
  method coordinate_propose_lez_vault_init(definition: tstr) -> tstr description "Propose initializing the LEZ multisig's vault as a holding of the token whose definition account is `definition` (hex or base58): InitializeAccount on the token program, with the vault as the authorized PDA. Once executed, the vault can receive and send that token."
  method lez_member_account(index: tstr) -> tstr description "One of THIS instance's LEZ member accounts (exo-3c9). Its key is derived in the keystore from the label lez-member/<index> and never exported. A LEZ multisig needs every member account fresh at create, so a member hands this id to whoever creates the multisig. With an index (0-63), that account. Empty: the first one the chain still shows fresh (read through Settings → lez-rpc). Returns JSON {index, account (hex), base58, fresh (true | false | null when the sequencer is unreachable)}, or {error}."
  method lez_multisig_create(threshold: tstr, members: tstr) -> tstr description "Create a k-of-n LEZ multisig ON CHAIN (exo-3c9). members is a comma-separated list of fresh member accounts, hex or base58. The program is Settings → lez-multisig-program, by default the build deployed on the LEZ testnet (logos-co/lez-multisig#45), with a random create key. The transaction is unsigned (anyone may create) and is sent to the user's sequencer without waiting on a block. In a joined room, the multisig is disclosed there automatically once the chain holds its state (the coordinate_intents tick). Returns JSON {family, chain, address (the state PDA), config, threshold, members, tx, label, pending: \"create\"}, which is also what coordinate_disclose_account takes, or {error: bad-member | bad-threshold | sequencer-unreachable | refused, detail}."
  method lez_pending() -> tstr description "The LEZ steps this instance has sent and the chain has not yet included (exo-3c9): create, propose, vote, or the settling Execute. Also the last outcomes. Returns JSON {pending: [{kind, intentId, index, since, room}], recent: [{kind, intentId, index, outcome, at}]}. A step still not included after 10 minutes is dropped (outcome \"timed out\"), which is how the chain refuses."
  method lez_msig_open(spec_json: tstr) -> tstr description "Open a lez-msig ceremony in the joined room (exo-0d3): an M-of-N multisig whose program verifies the members' approvals itself, at execution. spec_json is {threshold, members? (room identities, hex; default: every member of the room, the opener included), roles? ({identity: [propose|approve|execute]}; default all three), weights? ({identity: n}; default 1), timelockMs? (default 0), openExecution? (default true), accounts? ({identity: key hex}: an account that member disclosed in the room, picked as their approval key instead of a fresh one; it counts as their join, exo-1f9), private? (default false: a group-owned private PDA, exo-efa — its group secret is sealed to each member, its state is a note only they read, and its transactions are proved by the lez_msig_module, which must be installed)}. The program is Settings → lez-msig-program and the zone Settings → lez-chain. Every invited member's client joins on its own intents tick with a fresh approval key for this multisig, derived in its keystore and proved by its own signature; once all have joined, the opener's client sends Create from its own funded LEZ account (lez_member_account 0) and discloses the account into the room. A private one is created through the prover (signed by nobody), and its note is sealed into the room once the chain includes it. Returns JSON {createKey, chain, members, threshold, private}, or {error: not-joined | no-program | no-prover (with install) | bad-spec | bad-threshold | refused, detail}."
  method coordinate_disclose_my_account(spec_json: tstr) -> tstr description "Disclose an account of YOUR OWN to the joined room, so members can use it wherever an account of yours is needed: first as your approval key when the room sets up a lez-msig (exo-1f9). spec_json is {kind? (lez-key, the only kind yet), chain? (default Settings → lez-chain), which? (new: a fresh account from your keystore, the default | payer: your paying account, lez_member_account 0), name? (a label the room sees)}. The account key signs (room, you, chain, kind, key), so it is provably yours and only in this room, and the disclosure is author-signed; its keystore label is never disclosed. Reusing one account in several multisigs links them on chain. Returns {member, mine, id (its address, hex), chain, kind, name, key, base58}, or {error: not-joined | bad-spec | unknown-kind | already-disclosed | refused, detail}."
  method coordinate_member_accounts() -> tstr description "The accounts members disclosed to the joined room and still stand by (exo-1f9), in the room's canonical order: [{member (room identity), memberName, mine, id, chain, kind, name, key, base58}]. A disclosure whose proof does not verify, or whose key another member disclosed first, is not listed."
  method coordinate_retract_my_account(account_id: tstr) -> tstr description "Take back an account you disclosed to the joined room (exo-1f9); account_id is its id (address hex). It stops being offered; a multisig already set up with it keeps it. Returns {retracted}, or {error: not-joined | unknown-account | refused, detail}."
  method lez_msig_ceremonies() -> tstr description "The lez-msig ceremonies in the joined room (exo-0d3): [{createKey, chain, opener, openedByMe, members: [{id, joined, key}], threshold, timelockMs, openExecution, ready, invited, joined, createSent, account (its CAIP-10 once disclosed, else \"\")}]."
  method coordinate_propose_lez_msig(action_json: tstr) -> tstr description "Propose an action from the room's lez-msig account (policy lez-msig@<CAIP-10>, exo-0d3). action_json is {kind: transfer, to, amount} | {kind: call, program, rows: [{account, program}], data (hex)} | {kind: config, changes: [{change: add-member|remove-member|set-roles|set-weight|set-threshold|set-timelock|set-open-execution|add-limit|remove-limit, …}]} | {kind: cancel, nonces: [n]}, with an optional expiresInS (default 7 days). The proposal names the multisig's epoch read from the chain and the next nonce free on chain and in the room; the effect is its exact bytes, which the card decodes. Proposing is the proposer's approval, signed with their approval key for this multisig. Nothing touches the chain until coordinate_submit, which sends one transaction carrying the proposal and its approvals (Execute, or Queue then ExecuteQueued under a time lock). Returns JSON {intentId, nonce, epoch, approval, proposal}, or {error: not-joined | not-a-lez-msig-policy | bad-action | not-a-proposer | not-on-chain | sequencer-unreachable | refused, detail}."
  method lez_msig_fund(account: tstr, amount: tstr) -> tstr description "Deposit amount (base units) into a PRIVATE lez-msig's vault from this member's own LEZ account, lez_member_account 0 (exo-efa). The lez_msig_module proves it, this member's keystore signs it, the module sends it; once the chain includes it the new note is sealed to every member. account is its CAIP-10 id (empty: the room's lez-msig policy). A public multisig's vault takes any LEZ transfer to its address, so it answers {error: public}. Returns {job, amount, payer}, or {error: not-joined | not-a-lez-msig-policy | unknown-account | does-not-derive | public | bad-amount | refused, detail}."
  method lez_msig_recover(account: tstr) -> tstr description "Read a PRIVATE lez-msig's newest note from the chain (exo-abc): the lez_msig_module decrypts each block's private actions with the group's viewing key. For when the member whose transaction landed went away before sealing its note into the room; the found note is sealed to every member. Sends nothing, and also starts by itself when a private settlement fails. account is its CAIP-10 id (empty: the room's lez-msig policy). Returns {job}, or {error: not-joined | not-a-lez-msig-policy | unknown-account | does-not-derive | public | refused, detail}."
  method lez_msig_state(account: tstr) -> tstr description "The lez-msig multisig's state as its program holds it, read from the user's sequencer, or for a private one from its newest note (exo-0d3, exo-efa): {account, private, epoch, nonceBase, balance (the vault), config: {members: [{key, roles, weight}], threshold, timelockMs, openExecution, transfer, call, targets, limits}, myKey (this member's approval key for it), queue: [{nonce, proposalId, etaMs, expiresMs}], height}. account is its CAIP-10 id (empty: the room's lez-msig policy). Returns {error: not-joined | unknown-account | does-not-derive | not-on-chain | sequencer-unreachable} otherwise."

  method coordinate_accounts() -> tstr description "The accounts members have disclosed into the joined room (docs/design/multisig-landscape.md S1, exo-a50.1.3): a JSON array of {id (CAIP-10), family, chain (CAIP-2), address, label, signers, threshold, disclosedBy:[member identity], disclosedByAlias, conflict, check:{status, detail}}. An account exists to the room only once a member discloses it; conflict is true when members disclosed different signers or thresholds for it (the first in canonical order governs). check is whether the CHAIN agrees with the disclosure, read through the user's RPC: verified, disagrees (naming what differs), or unknown (why, e.g. the RPC serves another chain). Never a false verified."
  method coordinate_disclose_account(account_json: tstr) -> tstr description "Disclose an account into the joined room as this member (exo-a50.1.3). account_json is {family (evm.safe | btc.p2wsh-sortedmulti | btc.tapscript-multi-a | lez.multisig-program), chain (CAIP-2: eip155:<id>, bip122:<genesis>, lez:<zone>), address, label, signers?, threshold?, config? (a LEZ multisig: {program, createKey, pda, layout}; its address must be the state PDA the config derives, and its members and threshold are read from the chain through Settings → lez-rpc)}. For a Safe, omitted signers/threshold are read from the chain, and the disclosure is refused if they cannot be (never guessed). For a Bitcoin account, signers (33-byte keys) and threshold are required and the address is DERIVED from them — a given address must match (exo-a50.2.3). Publishes an account/<id>/disclose/<member> event and returns the account as folded, or {error}. Account-bound policies (safe, eip191) then act from it: coordinate_set_policy(\"safe@<id>\")."
  method coordinate_drivers() -> tstr description "Every driver kind this client has, from the one kind list (drivers/kinds.nim, exo-a50.1.2), with whether the joined room admits it (driver-as-proposal, invariant 6): a JSON array of {kind, family, label, composes:[payment|statement|action|split], founding, accountFamilies, settlesOn, admitted, needs}. needs is what the kind asks of THIS instance before anything is proposed under it: its module requirements, each graded exactly as a coordinate_readiness module item ({kind, name, party, needs, status, detail, remedy, moduleState, install}), so the composer can offer installing a missing module rather than hide the kind (exo-dcc.10); [] when the kind names none up front (an account-bound kind names its needs once a proposal names the account). Founding kinds are admitted in every room; any other is admitted only by an add-driver governance intent the room approved, and only if it is on the list. A kind not on the list is never offered and never resolved to another family: its intents fold as unsupported."

  method coordinate_set_policy(kind: tstr) -> tstr description "Choose this instance's compose default policy (its driver) for the next proposal. A kind from the one kind list (coordinate_drivers) that the room admits. A room kind (threshold, frost, invoke, unanimous) is its bare name. An account-bound kind (safe, eip191) acts FROM an account a member disclosed into the room (exo-a50.1.3): pass \"safe@<CAIP-10>\", or the bare kind when the room holds exactly one account it can use. Errors: not admitted; {error: no-account} when none is disclosed; {error: choose-account, accounts} when several are. Returns {policy, kind, account, threshold, domain}."

  method coordinate_policy() -> tstr description "The joined room's current coordination policy as {policy, threshold, domain}, described by its driver."

  method coordinate_request_join() -> tstr description "Ask to be admitted to the joined conversation: send a join request (our encryption identity, signed by that identity for this room, and nothing else: no secp key or address, exo-a87) sealed to each join key the room has announced on the topic, so only its members can read who is asking; a store node sees that someone asked, not who (FS-7, exo-661.7). This carries no authority — an existing member still has to admit us; it is discovery, not entry. Returns \"ok\" when the request was sent, or \"waiting-for-room-key\" when no member has announced a join key yet — the call asked them to, so call again shortly (the UI re-asks every few seconds)."

  method coordinate_pending() -> tstr description "List the encryption identities that have requested to join the conversation but have not been admitted, as a JSON array of 64-byte hex identities (ed25519 ++ x25519). A join-request confers nothing; a member reviews these and decides whom to admit."

  method coordinate_admit(identity_hex: tstr) -> tstr description "Admit a member (a 64-byte encryption identity hex: ed25519 ++ x25519) to the joined conversation: re-key the room forward and publish the grants so the joiner receives the new epoch key. Each grant is sealed whole to one member, so the topic carries one anonymous box per member of the new epoch and no roster (exo-661.7). The joiner gets only the new epoch, never earlier ones (F-16, membership epochs). Admits only an identity whose request is for this room, unexpired, and signed by the identity it names. Returns \"ok\", or \"not-joined\"/\"bad-key\"/\"unverified\"."

  method coordinate_post_message(body: tstr) -> tstr description "Post an authored event to the joined conversation over encrypted transport. `body` is either a plain chat message or a typed card as JSON {kind, ...} (address-share, receipt, fact); everything the room says rides one channel and the UI folds cards out of it (cards are data). Returns the event id. Adds a message event to the shared, sealed log (state = reduce(log), F-1/F-2)."

  method coordinate_messages() -> tstr description "The joined conversation's authored events, oldest-first, as a JSON array of {id, author, ts, body} folded from the shared log. `body` is the string posted — plain text or a typed card. Drives inbound delivery first, then reduces, so it reflects events that arrived from other participants; it is the fold source the UI reduces into the thread, the journey, and the home surface."

  method coordinate_members() -> tstr description "The admitted members of the joined conversation, as a JSON array of {identity, self} — 64-byte encryption identities (ed25519 ++ x25519), self flagged for this module's own. Distinct from coordinate_pending (join-requests not yet admitted): this is the current roster, who can read the room."

  method coordinate_conversations() -> tstr description "The conversations this identity has joined, as a JSON array of {topic, title, address, lastTs, people (the other members' names), active, needs, waiting, settled} — the home surface's room list, one entry per joined room (multi-room). Drives inbound delivery first so lastTs reflects recent activity. The home surface is a query over intents (F-18, exo-ed5): needs is [{id, what, state, text}] — each intent in that room waiting on THIS member (what: agree | pay | approve | submit), classed from the room's log and this member's own keys, and only when the intent's driver says this member's contribution would count; waiting and settled count the rest. Identity-inbox topics (coordinate_start_inbox) are excluded — an inbox is a drop-box, not a room."

  method security_levels() -> tstr description "The joined room's ACTIVE security level on the null ladder's three axes (exo-1ec.5), as JSON {axes:[{axis,rung,real,mechanism}]} — authentication, provenance, confidentiality, each the strongest guarantee an active seam provides with its named mechanism. Authentication is the compose-default driver's (every driver binds the speaker); provenance is the signed hash-linked log (reduce(log)); confidentiality is the room's crypto seam (the real ECIES epoch layer, F-16, or the null). Returns \"not-joined\" until a room is joined. A UI shows this as which nulls are still in place; the level is never a silent fallback — a failed upgrade refuses (DowngradeRefused)."

  method coordinate_start_inbox() -> tstr description "Begin listening on THIS identity's inbox topic — a deterministic content topic derived from this module's chat id — so room invites (coordinate_invite) arrive even before any room is joined — and re-enter every room this identity joined before a relaunch (exo-ecbe), so coordinate_conversations lists them again; none is made active. Idempotent; call once at startup. Pulls any invites left in the store while offline. Returns JSON {inbox, rooms}: rooms, the topics this call re-entered (empty after the first call)."
  method coordinate_invite(peer_chat_id_hex: tstr, room_topic: tstr, note: tstr) -> tstr description "Invite a peer to a room WITHOUT prior coordination: seal {topic, from, note, ts} to the peer's X25519 (a sealed box — no shared membership needed) and drop it on the peer's inbox topic (derived from their 64-byte chat id, ed25519++x25519 hex). The peer need not be online; the store retains it until coordinate_invites picks it up. Discloses only the room topic, and only to the named recipient. When this identity is a member of that room, the invite also ADMITS the peer (exo-dcc.29): naming them is the membership decision, so they arrive a member with no join request, reading from the invite on (F-16). Returns JSON {ok, inbox, admitted} or {error}."
  method coordinate_invites() -> tstr description "The room invites this identity has received, newest-first, as a JSON array of {topic, from, fromAlias, note, ts}. Each was sealed to us on our inbox topic; ones not ours or malformed are skipped; deduped by (from, topic). Invites for a room already joined, and ones the user dismissed (coordinate_dismiss_invite), are omitted. Empty until coordinate_start_inbox has been called. The home surface renders these as 'X invited you to a room' with Join and Dismiss actions."
  method coordinate_dismiss_invite(room_topic: tstr) -> tstr description "Clear a received room invite so it stops showing on the home surface (the user isn't joining that room), keyed by the normalized content topic. In-memory for the session; joining a room also drops its invite automatically. Returns \"ok\"."
  method coordinate_leave(room_topic: tstr) -> tstr description "Leave a room (exo-dcc.28): stop following it on this device, forget it so a relaunch does not re-enter it, and drop its name. Its session takes no more of the room's traffic; the delivery node keeps running for the rooms still joined. Nothing is announced and nothing re-keys — the others still count this member, and joining the topic again reads the room back. Leaving the active room leaves none active. Returns \"ok\" or \"not-joined\"."
  method coordinate_set_room_title(room_topic: tstr, title: tstr) -> tstr description "Name a room for yourself (exo-dcc.25): a room's topic names nothing, so its name is this member's own label, kept on this device beside the joined rooms and never sent to the room. coordinate_conversations returns it as `title`. An empty title clears it. Returns \"ok\"."

  method fileshare_status() -> tstr description "FileShare (the room swarm, exo-4d0; docs/design/room-swarm.md): whether this instance can share and fetch files. JSON {ready, note, node: {peerId, addrs, network, spr}}. ready is false, with a note saying why, when storage_module is not installed, not running, or its node is still starting. Muster attaches to the host's storage node and never stops it; it starts one only where nothing else has (the standalone runner)."
  method fileshare_share(path: tstr) -> tstr description "Share the file at `path` (absolute) into the joined room (FileShare, exo-4d0). The file is sealed under a fresh key (crypto/blob_seal.nim), uploaded to this instance's storage node under a neutral name, and — once the node holds it — announced in the room as an author-signed share carrying its name, size, digest, ciphertext CID and key (sealed to the room's current epoch), with this member's seed event. Storage never sees the file, its name or its key. It does not wait on the upload: returns JSON {op} at once, or {error: not-joined | not-ready | detail}; fileshare_files reports the op."
  method fileshare_files() -> tstr description "The joined room's shared files and this instance's file operations (FileShare, exo-4d0), folded from the log (reduceSwarm over the room's authentic view). JSON {ready, note, files: [{id, name, mime, size, sharer, sharerAlias, self, retracted, seeders: [{member, alias, self}], seeding, held}], ops: [{id, kind, state, error, file, name, bytes, total, dest, private}]}. A member is listed as seeding only by their own signed announce (invariant 9). Drives the pending operations."
  method fileshare_fetch(file_id: tstr, folder: tstr, privately: tstr) -> tstr description "Fetch a shared file into `folder` (absolute; empty = ~/Downloads) under its shared name, beside and never over what is there (FileShare, exo-4d0). It dials the seeders the room names, downloads the sealed blob, and saves it only if it is exactly what the sharer committed; a blob that is not is refused, dropped and never served. Unless privately is \"true\" this member then seeds it, announced in the room. A private fetch (\"true\") goes over Mix (slow: about 15 KB/s), dials no seeder and announces nothing. Returns JSON {op} at once, or {error}."
  method fileshare_seed(file_id: tstr, on: tstr) -> tstr description "Start (on = \"true\") or stop seeding a shared file this node holds, and say so in the room with an author-signed seed event (FileShare, exo-4d0). Stopping cannot withdraw the storage network's provider record at once: it expires. Returns \"ok\" or JSON {error}."
  method fileshare_retract(file_id: tstr) -> tstr description "Hide a file this member shared from the room's list (FileShare, exo-4d0). Only its sharer can. It cannot unshare: members who fetched it keep it, and its key stays in the room's log. Returns \"ok\" or JSON {error}."
  method contacts() -> tstr description "The address book: a JSON array of {identity, alias, address}. Each identity is a 64-byte encryption identity hex (ed25519 ++ x25519, no 0x) — the same id coordinate_admit/members speak — with the alias you gave it and an optional secp address. Persisted beside the keystore, so it survives restarts; coordinate_members and coordinate_pending resolve their aliases against it so the roster shows names, not raw hex."
  method contact_add(identity_hex: tstr, alias: tstr) -> tstr description "Add or update a contact: name a 64-byte encryption identity hex (0x optional). An empty alias does not clobber a stored one (so admitting can seed a contact a later rename fills in). Returns \"ok\"."
  method contact_set_alias(identity_hex: tstr, alias: tstr) -> tstr description "Rename a contact (or create it with just an alias). Returns \"ok\"."
  method contact_remove(identity_hex: tstr) -> tstr description "Remove a contact from the address book by its identity hex. Returns \"ok\"."
  method wallet_accounts() -> tstr description "The module's accounts across every configured chain, as a JSON array of {chain, form, id}. A chain may offer more than one form — a public id and a shielded key-set — from the one module identity. Settlement is chain-agnostic; a chain is a registered adapter, not a hardcoded assumption."

  method wallet_balances() -> tstr description "A balance snapshot across accounts and assets, as a JSON array of {chain, account, asset, display, raw} — or {chain, account, asset, error} when a read could not be answered. A failed read is reported as an error and NEVER as a zero balance, because a chain that fails by returning empty (the LEZ convention) must not be mistaken for an empty account. Balances are read locally against the user's endpoint (invariant 8, F-10 verified-locally)."
  method wallet_summary() -> tstr description "The Wallet page (exo-f37): the wallets this instance has been GIVEN, never a default. {wallets: [{id, name, account, via, dev?, network?, wallet?, state?, watchOnly?, balances: [{chain, account, asset, grade, display?, raw?, unlocked?, pending?, error?}]}], connect: [{id, name, how, action: accounts|settings|monero|install, package?}], checking: [name]}. Under the platform Ethereum is only the keystore_module account chosen for Muster (never Muster's own key); Monero is the wallet open in Monero Wallet (monero_wallet_backend, asked only when the host reports it running, through the cached pump: a read not answered yet is checking or pending, never zero), with its primary address and account 0's balance; the LEZ as the wallet reads it. In the standalone runner the instance's own key and demo chain, marked dev."

  method wallet_estimate_fee(chain: tstr, from_id: tstr, to: tstr, asset_symbol: tstr, raw: tstr) -> tstr description "Estimate the fee to transfer `raw` base units of the asset on `chain` to `to`, from `from_id` (an account wallet_accounts lists; empty = the module's account there). On the LEZ the source's form picks the rail, so a preview from a shielded account names the private rail and what it discloses, as the send will (exo-408 item 14). Returns JSON {fee, raw, note} (the note is chain-described — gas on EVM, a proof cost on a shielded chain), or {error}."

  method wallet_send(chain: tstr, from_id: tstr, to: tstr, asset_symbol: tstr, raw: tstr) -> tstr description "Send `raw` base units of the asset on `chain` from `from_id` to `to`. Prepares and submits the transfer (signing goes through the keystore/Keycard seam, FS-4). Returns JSON {txId, chain} for polling finality, or {error}. This is the account-level transfer, distinct from the coordinated multi-party intent path (propose/approve/submit)."

  method wallet_lez_setup() -> tstr description "Provision a LEZ account for THIS instance, headless, directly over lez_core (exo-44b, the no-broker FALLBACK). Ensures a public LEZ account exists and says whether it holds native LEZ. It funds nothing: LEZ v0.3 has no faucet and no account registration (exo-eb6.4), and a fresh public account is claimed on chain by its first funded transfer. Returns JSON {account, state, detail}: state is met (it holds native LEZ), missing (unfunded; detail names the account someone who holds native LEZ must send to) or unknown (the zone could not be read), per the lez-account readiness. This is muster driving lez_core by name (the sanctioned core-to-core pattern), so it needs neither the Basecamp app-to-app broker nor the LEZ Wallet App. It is the FALLBACK, not the default: when the app-to-app hand-off is available, delegating account setup to the first-party LEZ Wallet App stays preferred (better UX; keys in one home). The account keys stay in lez_core."

  method wallet_finality(chain: tstr, tx_id: tstr) -> tstr description "Poll the finality of a submitted transfer. Returns JSON {status, detail} where status is pending/final/failed — observed from the chain, never asserted (R-8). A delayed chain stays pending until settlement really completes; a balance read right after a send can be stale."

  method support_options() -> tstr description "Like Muster? (exo-168): the project's tip addresses, pinned in the module (wallet/support.json, published on the Pages site, never fetched), each valid on its own network, as JSON {prompt: unseen|dismissed, connected, about, options: [{id, chain, name, testnet, address, for, uri, discloses {payer, amount, note}, senders: [{wallet, walletName, account, asset, display, raw, form?, discloses}]}]}. `senders` are the balances a wallet the person connected holds on that chain (never a development wallet, never Monero: Muster never spends it; a Monero or Bitcoin tip is its link, paid from any wallet). `discloses` says who sees the payment. `connected` is whether any wallet the person connected is there, so the view can offer the prompt once; `prompt` is the support-prompt setting. Nothing here is reported anywhere."

  method support_send(option_id: tstr, from_id: tstr, asset_symbol: tstr, amount: tstr) -> tstr description "Send a tip (exo-168): `amount` (a decimal, e.g. 0.01) of the asset from `from_id` to the pinned address of option `option_id`, as support_options offered it — the caller never names the address, and a sender support_options did not offer is refused. Goes through wallet_send (under Basecamp an EVM tip is prepared by tx_sender_module and approved in the person's Signer; on the LEZ the rail follows the sender's form). Returns wallet_send's JSON plus {option, raw}, or {error}."

  method wallet_verified_balance(account_id: tstr, state_root_hex: tstr) -> tstr description "An EVM balance VERIFIED against a trusted state root (F-10 verified-locally): fetch eth_getProof from the untrusted RPC and check the account's Merkle proof against state_root_hex. Returns JSON {display, raw, grade: \"verified-locally\"} on a valid proof, or {error} if the proof does not verify (the provider is inconsistent with consensus — never trusted). Unlike wallet_balances (grade \"attested\"), this trusts only the state root; supply one from a beacon light-client sidecar (the Nimbus verified proxy)."
}
